17:30:53 [1d85] [StoryGenerator] prompt:  Write a short illustrated-style story for a 5-year-old female named sophie, who has speech delay. The story will be used by a robot to practise WH-question comprehension (WHO / WHAT / WHERE) with the child.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
17:30:53 [1d85] Match the style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these reference stories EXACTLY. Each is a 3–4 sentence concrete vignette in the present tense, followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers appear verbatim in the story.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Example 1 (setting: mud / dirt):
17:30:53 [1d85]   Story: The boys are playing in the mud. They are pretending to dig for buried treasure. A boy named Ian finds something. He puts it into his bucket. "Did you find treasure?" the boys ask. "It's not treasure," says Jack, "it's a snail!"
17:30:53 [1d85]   Questions:
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHERE are the boys playing?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHAT are they pretending to dig for?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHO finds something?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHERE does he put it?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHO asks Ian if he found treasure?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHAT did Ian find?
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Example 2 (setting: music class):
17:30:53 [1d85]   Story: The children are in music class. They are singing a song. The class next door is playing basketball. It is very loud! The children cannot hear their song. They start to sing louder.
17:30:53 [1d85]   Questions:
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHERE are the children?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHAT are they doing?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHERE is the other class?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHAT is the other class playing?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHO cannot hear the song?
17:30:53 [1d85]   - WHAT do the children do?
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
17:30:53 [1d85] Language level: Write 3–4 short, concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small everyday scene with a tiny twist or surprise — NOT a three-act journey. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
17:30:53 [1d85] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
17:30:53 [1d85] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
17:30:53 [1d85] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- VOCABULARY FOCUS ---
17:30:53 [1d85] Use rich descriptive vocabulary appropriate to the setting and characters.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
17:30:53 [1d85] - Collaboration and turn-taking
17:30:53 [1d85] - Importance of friendships and relationships
17:30:53 [1d85] - Overcoming challenges with support
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
17:30:53 [1d85] Target developmental level: age 5, approx MLU 6-8 words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
17:30:53 [1d85] - plural forms (Regular plural nouns): e.g. dogs, cars, apples, cats, toys
17:30:53 [1d85] - ing form (Present participle/progressive verb form): e.g. running, jumping, eating, playing
17:30:53 [1d85] - pronouns later (Later developing pronouns): e.g. he, she, they, her, his, their
17:30:53 [1d85] - location noun (Location concept expressed with a noun phrase): e.g. in the box, on the table, under the chair
17:30:53 [1d85] - simple conjunction (Early conjunction use): e.g. and, or
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Use these interest themes as story hooks, characters, and settings:
17:30:53 [1d85] - fairies (fairy, magic wand, wings); princesses (princess, castle, crown); pets (dog, cat, hamster, fish); superheroes (superhero, cape, rescue mission)
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
17:30:53 [1d85] - Warm, simple, and concrete. Describe one small everyday scene with a tiny surprise or twist.
17:30:53 [1d85] - sophie should appear by name in the story (not just "she" / "he"), so WHO-questions are answerable.
17:30:53 [1d85] - Use observable facts only (who is there, where they are, what they are doing, what they see/find).
17:30:53 [1d85] - Do NOT invent moral lessons, internal monologue, or three-act structure. Keep it to the corpus style.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] --- ROBOT GESTURES AND EMOTIONS ---
17:30:53 [1d85] A robot will read this aloud and act it out. Embed gesture or emotion tags INLINE in the story so the robot's face and body match the narrative. Keep these SPARSE (at most 2–3 tags total) so the story stays short.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Available gestures: [gesture:NAME] where NAME ∈ {hi, bye, nodding-yes, clapping, hoora, happy, calm, shy, embrace, patience, slight_no, think, sneezing, yawn, breathing_exercise, kiss, stretching}
17:30:53 [1d85] Available emotions: [emotion:NAME] where NAME ∈ {QT/happy, QT/sad, QT/surprised, QT/afraid, QT/angry, QT/calm, QT/shy}
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Place a tag IMMEDIATELY before the sentence that depicts the emotion or gesture. Do NOT include tags inside the WH-questions.
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] Return your answer EXACTLY in this format, with no other text before or after:
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] ** Title **
17:30:53 [1d85] <one short title>
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] <the full story text — 3 to 4 short concrete sentences in the present tense>
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] ** End **
17:30:53 [1d85] ** Questions **
17:30:53 [1d85] 1. <WH-question>
17:30:53 [1d85] 2. <WH-question>
17:30:53 [1d85] 3. <WH-question>
17:30:53 [1d85] 4. <WH-question>
17:30:53 [1d85] 5. <WH-question>
17:30:53 [1d85] (6. and 7. optional)
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] ** Explanation of the output **
17:30:53 [1d85] <1–2 short sentences explaining how the story and questions match the selected topic(s) and the learning goals: general speech-language therapy goals>
17:30:53 [1d85] 
17:30:53 [1d85] STRICT RULES:
17:30:53 [1d85] - Do NOT include any preamble, commentary, or meta-text (no "Here is your story", "Sure!", etc.).
17:30:53 [1d85] - The story text (between Title and End) MUST be between 40 and 70 words.
17:30:53 [1d85] - Generate 5–7 WH-questions using ONLY WHO / WHAT / WHERE (no HOW / WHY at this age tier).
17:30:53 [1d85] - Every question's answer MUST appear word-for-word in the story text. Do NOT ask about anything not stated.
17:30:53 [1d85] - Refer to characters by name (not pronouns) in the story so questions like "WHO is in the story?" are answerable.
17:30:53 [1d85] - Do NOT add sections beyond Title, story text, End, Questions, and Explanation.
