17:35:47 [2987] 1. BEGINNING: Introduce ethan, the setting, and ethan's goal or desire.
17:35:47 [2987] 2. MIDDLE: ethan encounters an obstacle. ethan meets a supportive character who helps. Show the process of overcoming the challenge together.
17:35:47 [2987] 3. END: ethan achieves the goal, learns something, and feels positive about the experience.
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] --- VOCABULARY AND LANGUAGE TARGETS ---
17:35:47 [2987] Use rich descriptive vocabulary appropriate to the setting and characters.
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
17:35:47 [2987] - Collaboration and turn-taking
17:35:47 [2987] - Importance of friendships and relationships
17:35:47 [2987] - Overcoming challenges with support
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
17:35:47 [2987] Target developmental level: age 8, approx MLU 11+ words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
17:35:47 [2987] - irregular past tense (Irregular past tense verbs): e.g. went, ate, saw, gave
17:35:47 [2987] - advanced conjunction (More complex conjunction structures): e.g. while, although, before, after
17:35:47 [2987] - auxiliary verbs (Helping verbs): e.g. is, are, was, were, has, have
17:35:47 [2987] - copula verbs (Linking verbs): e.g. is, am, are, was, were
17:35:47 [2987] - verb tense concepts (Past, present, future, and progressive tense usage): e.g. I play, I played, I will play, I am playing
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] Use these interest themes as story hooks, characters, and settings:
17:35:47 [2987] - superheroes (superhero, cape, rescue mission); video game characters (game hero, game creature, game vehicle); vehicles (car, truck, bus, train, airplane)
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] --- TAKEAWAYS / LESSONS ---
17:35:47 [2987] The story MUST teach 2–3 clear takeaways that the child can articulate after listening. Demonstrate them through what characters DO and the consequences they face — do NOT preach or moralise inside the narration. After the story ends, list the takeaways explicitly in a ** Takeaways ** section.
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] Takeaway rules:
17:35:47 [2987] - Each takeaway is ONE short sentence a 7-year-old can repeat back in their own words.
17:35:47 [2987] - Phrase them as positive, actionable lessons or values — not as "do not" prohibitions when a positive form is natural.
17:35:47 [2987] - Each takeaway must connect to a specific moment or decision in the story (cause → consequence).
17:35:47 [2987] - Cover values like honesty, kindness, perseverance, asking for help, sharing, courage, patience, listening, or fairness — pick the ones that fit the plot.
17:35:47 [2987] 
17:35:47 [2987] Examples of well-formed takeaways:
17:35:47 [2987] - "Asking for help is a sign of being smart, not weak."
17:35:47 [2987] - "Being patient often works better than rushing."
17:35:47 [2987] - "Telling the truth is hard but builds trust."
17:35:47 [2987] - "Treating others kindly makes them want to help you back."
17:35:47 [2987] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
17:35:47 [2987] - Warm, encouraging, and gently paced.
17:35:47 [2987] - Show, don't tell: use actions and dialogue to convey emotions rather than stating them.
17:35:47 [2987] - Include at least one moment of humor, wonder, or sensory delight.
17:35:47 [2987] - Use character names consistently (avoid pronoun ambiguity for young readers).
17:35:47 [2987] 
