18:23:11 [09e9] [StoryGenerator] prompt:  Write a short illustrated-style story for a 5-year-old female named sophie, who has speech delay. The story will be used by a robot to practise WH-question comprehension (WHO / WHAT / WHERE) with the child.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
18:23:11 [09e9] Match the style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these reference stories EXACTLY. Each is a 3–4 sentence concrete vignette in the present tense, followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers appear verbatim in the story.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] Example 1 (setting: bus stop):
18:23:11 [09e9]   Story: The children are getting on the bus. Their friend Kate is not there yet. "Kate will miss the bus!" they say. "She cannot walk here in time." Suddenly they see Kate. She did not walk, she rode her fastest scooter!
18:23:11 [09e9]   Questions:
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHAT are the children getting on?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHO is not there yet?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHAT might Kate miss?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHO might miss the bus?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHO do the children see?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHAT is she riding?
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] Example 2 (setting: schoolyard):
18:23:11 [09e9]   Story: The children are in the schoolyard. They are playing. The boys are throwing a football. The girls are jumping rope. Oh no, rain clouds are coming! Soon the children will have to go inside.
18:23:11 [09e9]   Questions:
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHERE are the children?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHAT are they doing?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHO is playing football?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHAT are the girls doing?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHAT is coming?
18:23:11 [09e9]   - WHERE will the children go?
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
18:23:11 [09e9] Language level: Write 3–4 short, concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small everyday scene with a tiny twist or surprise — NOT a three-act journey. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
18:23:11 [09e9] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
18:23:11 [09e9] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
18:23:11 [09e9] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- VOCABULARY FOCUS ---
18:23:11 [09e9] Use rich descriptive vocabulary appropriate to the setting and characters.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
18:23:11 [09e9] - Collaboration and turn-taking
18:23:11 [09e9] - Importance of friendships and relationships
18:23:11 [09e9] - Overcoming challenges with support
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
18:23:11 [09e9] Target developmental level: age 5, approx MLU 6-8 words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
18:23:11 [09e9] - plural forms (Regular plural nouns): e.g. dogs, cars, apples, cats, toys
18:23:11 [09e9] - ing form (Present participle/progressive verb form): e.g. running, jumping, eating, playing
18:23:11 [09e9] - pronouns later (Later developing pronouns): e.g. he, she, they, her, his, their
18:23:11 [09e9] - location noun (Location concept expressed with a noun phrase): e.g. in the box, on the table, under the chair
18:23:11 [09e9] - simple conjunction (Early conjunction use): e.g. and, or
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
18:23:11 [09e9] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] Use these interest themes as story hooks, characters, and settings:
18:23:11 [09e9] - fairies (fairy, magic wand, wings); princesses (princess, castle, crown); pets (dog, cat, hamster, fish); superheroes (superhero, cape, rescue mission)
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
18:23:11 [09e9] - Warm, simple, and concrete. Describe one small everyday scene with a tiny surprise or twist.
18:23:11 [09e9] - sophie should appear by name in the story (not just "she" / "he"), so WHO-questions are answerable.
18:23:11 [09e9] - Use observable facts only (who is there, where they are, what they are doing, what they see/find).
18:23:11 [09e9] - Do NOT invent moral lessons, internal monologue, or three-act structure. Keep it to the corpus style.
18:23:11 [09e9] 
18:23:11 [09e9] --- ROBOT GESTURES AND EMOTIONS ---
18:23:11 [09e9] A robot will read this aloud and act it out. Embed gesture or emotion tags INLINE in the story so the robot's face and body match the narrative. Keep these SPARSE (at most 2–3 tags total) so the story stays short.
