19:34:49 [ec9e] The story MUST teach 2–3 clear takeaways that the child can articulate after listening. Demonstrate them through what characters DO and the consequences they face — do NOT preach or moralise inside the narration. After the story ends, list the takeaways explicitly in a ** Takeaways ** section.
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] Takeaway rules:
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Each takeaway is ONE short sentence a 7-year-old can repeat back in their own words.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Phrase them as positive, actionable lessons or values — not as "do not" prohibitions when a positive form is natural.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Each takeaway must connect to a specific moment or decision in the story (cause → consequence).
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Cover values like honesty, kindness, perseverance, asking for help, sharing, courage, patience, listening, or fairness — pick the ones that fit the plot.
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] Examples of well-formed takeaways:
19:34:49 [ec9e] - "Asking for help is a sign of being smart, not weak."
19:34:49 [ec9e] - "Being patient often works better than rushing."
19:34:49 [ec9e] - "Telling the truth is hard but builds trust."
19:34:49 [ec9e] - "Treating others kindly makes them want to help you back."
19:34:49 [ec9e] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Warm, encouraging, and gently paced.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Show, don't tell: use actions and dialogue to convey emotions rather than stating them.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Include at least one moment of humor, wonder, or sensory delight.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Use character names consistently (avoid pronoun ambiguity for young readers).
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] --- ROBOT GESTURES AND EMOTIONS ---
19:34:49 [ec9e] A robot will read this story aloud and physically act it out. Embed gesture or emotion tags INLINE in the story text so the robot's face and body match what is happening in the narrative.
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] Available gestures (use [gesture:NAME] format):
19:34:49 [ec9e]   hi, bye, nodding-yes, clapping, hoora, happy, calm, shy, embrace, patience,
19:34:49 [ec9e]   slight_no, think, sneezing, yawn, breathing_exercise, kiss, stretching
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] Available emotions (use [emotion:NAME] format) — use ONLY these exact names:
19:34:49 [ec9e]   QT/happy, QT/sad, QT/surprised, QT/afraid, QT/angry, QT/calm, QT/shy
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] Rules for tags:
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Tag EVERY clear emotional beat. Whenever a character smiles, laughs, giggles, or feels happy/proud/excited, insert [emotion:QT/happy]. Whenever they cry, frown, or feel sad/disappointed, insert [emotion:QT/sad]. Apply the same rule for surprised, afraid, angry, calm, and shy.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Place the tag IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the sentence that depicts the emotion or action — not at the start of the paragraph.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - It is fine to use the same emotion multiple times in one paragraph if the character feels it more than once.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Do NOT invent emotion names. If the feeling isn't in the list above, pick the closest available one (e.g. "relieved" or "proud" → QT/happy; "frustrated" → QT/angry; "nervous" → QT/afraid).
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Use gesture tags for physical actions (waving, clapping, nodding) where they fit the story.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Example: 'Anna looked at the puppy. [emotion:QT/happy] She smiled brightly and laughed.'
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Example: '[gesture:nodding-yes] [emotion:QT/happy] "Yes, I can help!" said the rabbit.'
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Example: 'The wind blew hard. [emotion:QT/surprised] Suddenly, a big rainbow appeared in the sky!'
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] Return your answer EXACTLY in this format, with no other text before or after:
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] ** Title **
19:34:49 [ec9e] <one short title>
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] <the full story text>
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] ** End **
19:34:49 [ec9e] ** Takeaways **
19:34:49 [ec9e] - <takeaway 1: one short, kid-friendly sentence stating a lesson the child should learn>
19:34:49 [ec9e] - <takeaway 2: another lesson the story demonstrates>
19:34:49 [ec9e] - <takeaway 3: another lesson (optional)>
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] ** Explanation of the output **
19:34:49 [ec9e] <1–3 short sentences explaining how the story matches the selected topic(s) and the learning goals: general speech-language therapy goals>
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
19:34:49 [ec9e] STRICT RULES:
19:34:49 [ec9e] - HARD WORD LIMIT: The story text between ** Title ** and ** End ** MUST be 130–200 words. 200 is a CEILING you cannot exceed. Target around 165 words so you have safety margin. Count as you write; if you approach 200, wrap up immediately at the next natural sentence — do not start a new subplot, paragraph, or piece of dialogue.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Brevity beats completeness. Cut adjectives, side details, and extra dialogue exchanges before going over.
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Do NOT include any preamble, commentary, or meta-text (no "Here is your story", "Sure!", etc.).
19:34:49 [ec9e] - Do NOT add sections beyond Title, story text, End, Takeaways, and Explanation.
19:34:49 [ec9e] Do not add any other sections or extra text.
19:34:49 [ec9e] 
