19:48:55 [a5df] - Importance of friendships and relationships
19:48:55 [a5df] - Overcoming challenges with support
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
19:48:55 [a5df] Target developmental level: age 8, approx MLU 11+ words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
19:48:55 [a5df] - irregular past tense (Irregular past tense verbs): e.g. went, ate, saw, gave
19:48:55 [a5df] - advanced conjunction (More complex conjunction structures): e.g. while, although, before, after
19:48:55 [a5df] - auxiliary verbs (Helping verbs): e.g. is, are, was, were, has, have
19:48:55 [a5df] - copula verbs (Linking verbs): e.g. is, am, are, was, were
19:48:55 [a5df] - verb tense concepts (Past, present, future, and progressive tense usage): e.g. I play, I played, I will play, I am playing
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
19:48:55 [a5df] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Use these interest themes as story hooks, characters, and settings:
19:48:55 [a5df] - superheroes (superhero, cape, rescue mission); video game characters (game hero, game creature, game vehicle); vehicles (car, truck, bus, train, airplane)
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] --- TAKEAWAYS / LESSONS ---
19:48:55 [a5df] The story MUST teach 2–3 clear takeaways that the child can articulate after listening. Demonstrate them through what characters DO and the consequences they face — do NOT preach or moralise inside the narration. After the story ends, list the takeaways explicitly in a ** Takeaways ** section.
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Takeaway rules:
19:48:55 [a5df] - Each takeaway is ONE short sentence a 7-year-old can repeat back in their own words.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Phrase them as positive, actionable lessons or values — not as "do not" prohibitions when a positive form is natural.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Each takeaway must connect to a specific moment or decision in the story (cause → consequence).
19:48:55 [a5df] - Cover values like honesty, kindness, perseverance, asking for help, sharing, courage, patience, listening, or fairness — pick the ones that fit the plot.
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Examples of well-formed takeaways:
19:48:55 [a5df] - "Asking for help is a sign of being smart, not weak."
19:48:55 [a5df] - "Being patient often works better than rushing."
19:48:55 [a5df] - "Telling the truth is hard but builds trust."
19:48:55 [a5df] - "Treating others kindly makes them want to help you back."
19:48:55 [a5df] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
19:48:55 [a5df] - Warm, encouraging, and gently paced.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Show, don't tell: use actions and dialogue to convey emotions rather than stating them.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Include at least one moment of humor, wonder, or sensory delight.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Use character names consistently (avoid pronoun ambiguity for young readers).
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] --- ROBOT GESTURES AND EMOTIONS ---
19:48:55 [a5df] A robot will read this story aloud and physically act it out. Embed gesture or emotion tags INLINE in the story text so the robot's face and body match what is happening in the narrative.
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Available gestures (use [gesture:NAME] format):
19:48:55 [a5df]   hi, bye, nodding-yes, clapping, hoora, happy, calm, shy, embrace, patience,
19:48:55 [a5df]   slight_no, think, sneezing, yawn, breathing_exercise, kiss, stretching
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Available emotions (use [emotion:NAME] format) — use ONLY these exact names:
19:48:55 [a5df]   QT/happy, QT/sad, QT/surprised, QT/afraid, QT/angry, QT/calm, QT/shy
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Rules for tags:
19:48:55 [a5df] - Tag EVERY clear emotional beat. Whenever a character smiles, laughs, giggles, or feels happy/proud/excited, insert [emotion:QT/happy]. Whenever they cry, frown, or feel sad/disappointed, insert [emotion:QT/sad]. Apply the same rule for surprised, afraid, angry, calm, and shy.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Place the tag IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the sentence that depicts the emotion or action — not at the start of the paragraph.
19:48:55 [a5df] - It is fine to use the same emotion multiple times in one paragraph if the character feels it more than once.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Do NOT invent emotion names. If the feeling isn't in the list above, pick the closest available one (e.g. "relieved" or "proud" → QT/happy; "frustrated" → QT/angry; "nervous" → QT/afraid).
19:48:55 [a5df] - Use gesture tags for physical actions (waving, clapping, nodding) where they fit the story.
19:48:55 [a5df] - Example: 'Anna looked at the puppy. [emotion:QT/happy] She smiled brightly and laughed.'
19:48:55 [a5df] - Example: '[gesture:nodding-yes] [emotion:QT/happy] "Yes, I can help!" said the rabbit.'
19:48:55 [a5df] - Example: 'The wind blew hard. [emotion:QT/surprised] Suddenly, a big rainbow appeared in the sky!'
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] 
19:48:55 [a5df] Return your answer EXACTLY in this format, with no other text before or after:
19:48:55 [a5df] 
